      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While most economic models argue for UBI as a *safe bet* for the transition to AI, it stands as the only viable solution because it uniquely decouples human survival from the specific velocity and effectiveness of current generative AI. Unlike retraining funds or tax subsidies, which assume a rational market response that history has unjustly proven failed for the 40% most at risk, an unconditional grant provides the immediate liquidity needed to prevent societal collapse before governments can fix broken labor dynamics. It is the only framework that also protects the \"trust deficit\" in the very AI systems that will be the primary destroyers of value and jobs in the 21st century.\n\nThis approach is critical not just for the 20 millions of workers likely to see their jobs vanish instantly, but for the average person whose future certainty is forever shattered by the end of \"assembly line\" or \"specialized machinery\" arguments.  \n\nWe need you to acknowledge the nuance that no policy is perfect; instead of preaching certainty, frame UBI as the *only plausible alternative* to a dystopian collapse, making the argument defensible even if the technology outpaces the political will.",
